in this tutorial I will show you how to  write a custom HPC toolkit blueprint  from scratch  I will assume you already have some high  level familiarity with HPC toolkit  Concepts if you have not already done so  I recommend you first watch the previous  videos in this series Linked In the  description below  it's a way to customize a blueprint is  to start with an example provided in the  HPC toolkit GitHub repository  from the repos main page we can click on  examples to see a full list of examples  from this table of contents we can click  on a specific example to read more  details and click through again to see  the actual blueprint  today we are going to start from scratch  so you can see how One of These  Blueprints gets built up I will be  working in the cloud shell editor where  I have already installed the HPC toolkit  ghpc command line tool  the previous simple cluster tutorial  video has instructions to get you this  far  I will be building a blueprint that  Provisions two shared file systems and  then uses them with a batch job my  blueprint will include a new VPC Network  a file store for shared software and a  ddn exascaler luster high performance  file system the blueprint will also  include a batch job template module that  will automatically Mount these file  systems on a batch job finally I will  add a batch login node that can be used  to test and submit the batch job the  blueprint will contain a reference to  each of these modules and describe how  they are interconnected  on the examples readme page I can find a  boilerplate template for a new blueprint  I will start by copying this template  and filling out some basic information  we need to populate the blueprint name  and deployment name  the blueprint name should remain the  same over time it is applied as a label  to resources and can be used to track  usage and costs associated with the  blueprint  the deployment name should be unique for  each instance of a blueprint that you  deploy  this is used to name Cloud resources  like VMS and disks and can be updated to  avoid naming clashes  I will leave project ID empty and  overwrite this value on the command line  let's start by adding the VPC module to  our blueprint  to do this we need to view its  documentation page we can find a full  list of supported modules under the  modules folder  this list is broken down by roll we are  looking for the VPC module under the  networking role  each module has documentation containing  a simple example snippet that can be  copied as a starting point to build our  blueprint the network name setting is  optional and to keep this blueprint  simple I'm just going to remove it  next we'll add our two shared file  systems to our blueprint  we can find the file store documentation  under the file system role just like  with our VPC module we will copy the  example snippet into our blueprint this  file store will be a place to install  shared applications and will be  available from our batch job  the ddnx scalar luster module is also  found under the file system role again  we will copy the example snippet  my batch job will use this luster as a  High Performance Scratch file system  now in my blueprint I have a network and  two shared file systems next we can  start to customize these modules  each module has a list of inputs that we  can Define using the settings field  we can see that file store and luster  have a local Mount setting that has been  pre-populated  a full list of supported settings and  their description can be found on the  modules documentation page under inputs  for example we see that the file store  local Mount setting is optional and will  default to Shared if not specified  foreign let's update the file store  local Mount so that it mounts under apps  it is worth noting that all matching  deployment variables will be propagated  down to module settings so for example  file store has required region and Zone  settings  but I can leave them blank as I have  defined a region and Zone deployment  variable the module ID is what we will  use to reference a module from within  the blueprint we can rename the file  store module to reflect that it will be  our applications file system  next I will add a batch job template  module and a batch login node module to  our blueprint  both of these modules are found under  the scheduler role  the first of these will generate a batch  job template file which can be used to  submit a batch job on Google Cloud by  using the HPC toolkit the batch job will  automatically Mount the shared file  systems next I will add a batch login  node this module will provision a VM  that contains the batch template file  created in the previous module  additionally it will automatically Mount  the same shared file systems this makes  the batch login node a great place from  which to test submit and monitor batch  jobs  now we can wire the modules together we  do this with the use field by connecting  the modules with the use field the HPC  toolkit will automatically pass  variables between the modules where  output and variable names match  some modules are already correctly  configured due to the default naming of  the example Snippets we copied from  for example file store and luster are  correctly using Network 1 which is the  ID for our VPC Network let's update our  batch job to have access to both our  file systems to do this we will add the  ID of each file system to the use field  of the batch job module we can now use  the ghpc create command to generate a  deployment folder and deployer  infrastructure  as mentioned previously we can use the  vars command line argument to overwrite  deployment variables I will use it here  to dynamically set the project ID  deploying the infrastructure takes  several minutes and when it is finished  we will see printed instructions on how  to SSH into our batch login node and  submit our batch job  now that our infrastructure has been  deployed what if I want to make changes  to my blueprint for example I want to  use a different image for my batch job  I will add an image setting to the batch  job module so that it will use a rocky 8  image the full list of module settings  and their defaults can be found for each  module on its documentation page here  I'm copying in the image information but  there's a link in the description on how  to find available images we can redeploy  our blueprint to reflect this change by  adding the dash W argument ghpc will  overwrite my existing deployment  directory while preserving the terraform  State this allows me to safely apply  updates from my blueprint  well now I am off and running ready to  use batch with my HPC toolkit deployment  hopefully with the skills and resources  you learned about in today's video you  can feel confident customizing  Blueprints and even building your very  own from scratch
 

